CAT = "cemapMarkets"; SEG = "MKT"
# (stem, key, [three wrong], explanation). The key is written first; build.py rotates its slot.
Q = [
("Which three functions does money perform in an economy?",
 "Exchange, store of value, unit of account", ["Exchange, credit and taxation of income", "Saving, lending and spending power", "Investment, interest payments and economic growth"],
 "Money is a medium of exchange, a store of value and a unit of account. Credit and taxation use money but are not its functions."),
("In which market are new shares first sold to investors by the company issuing them?",
 "The primary market", ["The secondary market", "The money market", "The foreign exchange market"],
 "New issues raise capital in the primary market. Later trading between investors happens in the secondary market, where the company receives nothing."),
("The money markets mainly deal in:",
 "Short-term lending and borrowing", ["Long-term lending through company shares", "Buying and selling commercial property", "Trading in physical commodities"],
 "Money markets handle wholesale lending and borrowing for periods of up to about a year, such as Treasury bills and interbank loans. Shares and property belong to capital markets."),
("Which body issues gilts on behalf of HM Treasury?",
 "The UK Debt Management Office", ["The Prudential Regulation Authority", "The Financial Conduct Authority", "The London Stock Exchange"],
 "The UK Debt Management Office, an executive agency of HM Treasury, issues gilts and Treasury bills. The Stock Exchange is where gilts are later traded."),
("When market interest rates rise, the price of an existing fixed-coupon bond usually:",
 "Falls", ["Rises", "Stays the same", "Doubles its coupon"],
 "A fixed coupon becomes less attractive when new bonds pay more, so the price of existing bonds falls until their yield matches the market. Prices and yields move in opposite directions."),
("Which feature is typical of preference shares compared with ordinary shares?",
 "A fixed dividend paid first", ["Full voting rights at every meeting", "Dividends that rise with profits", "Last claim on a winding up"],
 "Preference shareholders usually receive a fixed dividend before ordinary shareholders and rank ahead of them on a winding up, but normally have no vote."),
("Which asset class is generally the least liquid?",
 "Commercial property", ["Cash in a deposit account", "Shares listed on the exchange", "Conventional gilts"],
 "Selling a building can take months and costs are high, so property is illiquid. Cash is the most liquid, and listed shares and gilts trade daily."),
("Over long periods, which asset class has historically given the highest returns but with the greatest volatility?",
 "Equities", ["Cash", "Gilts", "Corporate bonds"],
 "Shares have tended to outperform cash and bonds over the long term because investors are paid for bearing more risk, which shows up as larger swings in value."),
("The main risk to the real value of money held in a deposit account over many years is:",
 "Inflation", ["Default of the gilt market", "Currency hedging", "Share price volatility"],
 "If interest earned is below inflation, the money buys less each year even though the balance never falls. Deposits are not exposed to share price swings."),
("Who sets the inflation target that the Bank of England must aim for?",
 "The Chancellor of the Exchequer", ["The Monetary Policy Committee", "The Financial Conduct Authority", "The Prudential Regulation Authority"],
 "The Chancellor sets the inflation target each year in the remit letter to the Bank of England. The Monetary Policy Committee then sets Bank Rate to meet it."),
("Which is a role of the Bank of England?",
 "Lender of last resort to banks", ["Authorising mortgage advisers", "Setting income tax rates", "Running the Financial Ombudsman Service"],
 "The Bank can lend to solvent banks that run short of cash, to protect the system. Advisers are authorised by the FCA, tax rates are set by Parliament, and the ombudsman is independent."),
("The Financial Policy Committee of the Bank of England is mainly concerned with:",
 "Risks to the whole financial system", ["The conduct of individual advisers", "Setting the government's annual Budget", "Handling consumer complaints"],
 "The FPC is the UK's macro-prudential authority: it identifies and acts on systemic risks, such as rising household debt. Adviser conduct is the FCA's concern."),
("Quantitative easing involves the Bank of England:",
 "Buying bonds with newly created money", ["Selling gilts to cut the money supply", "Lowering income tax for savers", "Lending directly to home buyers"],
 "Quantitative easing is monetary policy: the Bank creates reserves to buy gilts and other bonds, pushing down long-term interest rates. Selling them back is quantitative tightening."),
("A rise in Bank Rate is most likely to lead to:",
 "Higher variable mortgage rates", ["Lower rates on savings accounts", "Lower funding costs for lenders", "Higher prices for existing bonds"],
 "Lenders' costs follow Bank Rate, so tracker and standard variable rates usually rise with it. Savings rates tend to rise too, and bond prices tend to fall."),
("Which measure of inflation is used for the government's inflation target?",
 "The Consumer Prices Index", ["The Retail Prices Index (RPI)", "Average UK house price growth", "The FTSE 100 share index"],
 "The Bank of England's remit targets CPI inflation, measured by the Office for National Statistics. RPI is an older measure no longer used for the target."),
("In money laundering, the stage where criminal cash is first put into the financial system is called:",
 "Placement", ["Layering", "Integration", "Structuring"],
 "Placement gets the cash into a bank or other institution. Layering then moves it through transactions to hide its origin, and integration returns it as apparently clean funds."),
("Buying or selling shares using price-sensitive information that is not public is:",
 "Insider dealing", ["Front running orders", "Churning", "Short selling"],
 "Dealing on inside information is a criminal offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 and also market abuse under UK MAR. Churning is excessive trading to earn commission."),
("Which international body sets the standards that UK anti-money laundering rules follow?",
 "The Financial Action Task Force", ["The International Monetary Fund", "The World Trade Organization", "The Basel Committee"],
 "FATF sets global standards against money laundering and terrorist financing. The Basel Committee sets bank capital standards, and the IMF supports monetary stability."),
("Since the UK left the EU, the EU-derived financial services law kept on the statute book is being:",
 "Replaced by UK rules under FSMA 2023", ["Applied directly by the European Commission", "Enforced by EU regulators in the UK", "Left unchanged by Parliament"],
 "The Financial Services and Markets Act 2023 revokes retained EU law in financial services so that the Treasury, the FCA and the PRA can replace it with UK rules."),
("Bank capital standards that the PRA applies in the UK derive mainly from:",
 "The Basel accords", ["The FATF recommendations", "The Consumer Duty", "The Money Laundering Regulations"],
 "The Basel Committee's capital and liquidity standards, most recently Basel III, set how much capital banks must hold. The PRA implements them for UK banks."),
]
